WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:07.380
Mass disappearances, promiscuous priests?!&nbsp;
Critics can’t stop gobbling gabbagool — so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.420
are they breaking bad or are these&nbsp;
really the best television drama seasons?

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:19.140
Although "The Americans" finished strong, the&nbsp;
show's creative peak came during its 4th season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.160
as it was around this point in the&nbsp;
show that many of the central plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.420
points came to fruition. Specifically,&nbsp;
Philip's relationship with Martha,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.320
a government employee he's been quietly&nbsp;
siphoning information out of for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:37.080
finally comes undone when he is forced to frame&nbsp;
Gene, one of Martha's coworkers, for her spying.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:43.500
Martha's exfiltration causes a rift between Philip&nbsp;
and Elizabeth, and ultimately leads both of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.660
to question their loyalty to a country they&nbsp;
haven't set foot in for years. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.100
their daughter Paige is now fully aware that&nbsp;
her parents are Russian spies. By the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.960
the season, she's considering entering the&nbsp;
spy game herself. The season's final moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.520
emphasize the price that Philip and Elizabeth&nbsp;
have had to pay as a result of their profession.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:09.540
"The Americans" was always a grim show, but Season&nbsp;
4 spends a huge portion of its run time examining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.240
what being a spy does to a person's soul. Philip&nbsp;
and Elizabeth are hollow shells of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.067
and by the end of the season, they realize&nbsp;
that's not the fate they want for their daughter.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.920
"Don't do this, Paige."

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:23.420
"Dad!"

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.140
"You have no idea. No idea."

00:01:29.216 --> 00:01:33.540
The docu-drama series "The Staircase"&nbsp;
began in 2004 as an early contribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.280
to the now-wildly popular true crime genre.&nbsp;
It follows the trial of Michael Peterson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.920
who was convicted of murdering his wife, Kathleen.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.940
After a successful first season, the&nbsp;
showrunners published more installments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.620
based on updates in the case in 2013&nbsp;
and 2018. The miniseries, directed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.560
Jean-Xavier de Lestrade chronicles not only the&nbsp;
twists and turns of the original case, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.840
how corruptible the justice system is and the&nbsp;
horrible toll that prison can take on a person.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.780
While precious few miniseries provide viewers the&nbsp;
satisfaction of definitive proof regarding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.060
truth behind a crime, "The Staircase" relishes&nbsp;
in the ambiguous set of facts that surround the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.680
case. Whether or not Michael Peterson was&nbsp;
guilty of the crime he was convicted for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.480
his family and the audience must live&nbsp;
with the uncertainty about whether he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.159
deserved the punishment he ultimately&nbsp;
received for the crime he was convicted.

00:02:26.159 --> 00:02:28.380
It's always disappointing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:34.080
see shows as great as "Enlightened" get canceled,&nbsp;
especially after the show repeatedly achieved new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.240
heights throughout its 2nd and final season.&nbsp;
The series tells the story of Amy Jellicoe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.400
a career-oriented woman who has a breakdown&nbsp;
and ends up at Open Air, a rehabilitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.560
program in Hawaii. When she returns from the&nbsp;
program a sunnier, peppier version of herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.780
she discovers that reacclimating to her old life&nbsp;
might not be as straightforward as she expected.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:03:00.120
In Season 2, "Enlightened" transforms&nbsp;
Amy into an impassioned crusader whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.080
only goal is to take down the company&nbsp;
she once worked for. She spends the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.500
season working with a journalist&nbsp;
known for uncovering corruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.020
but ultimately discovers that the systems she's&nbsp;
trying to destroy are much harder to take down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:18.180
than she thought. Like so many people in modern&nbsp;
America, Amy wants to be an advocate for change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:25.560
but she's caught between her fierce idealism and&nbsp;
the intractable nature of modern capitalism. Huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.000
multinational companies just don't play&nbsp;
by the same rules as the rest of us.

00:03:30.745 --> 00:03:32.520
It was easy to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:37.320
dismissive of Noah Hawley's decision to adapt&nbsp;
"Fargo," the 1996 Coen Brothers masterpiece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.780
into a TV series of the same name. Even after the&nbsp;
1st season, it wasn't entirely clear how Hawley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.960
was going to transform this story into something&nbsp;
that could last for years. After Season 2, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.000
"Fargo" proved that fans of the original film&nbsp;
could stop worrying so much about the adaptation.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.640
The show's 2nd season dives back into the late&nbsp;
1970s, a time when many Americans felt like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:05.220
their country was in decline, to tell a story&nbsp;
of warring crime families and intergenerational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.720
conflict. The narrative is beautifully&nbsp;
paced through the season's 10 episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:15.600
and all of the actors know how to make a meal out&nbsp;
of the peculiar dialect that the show relies on.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.560
"Okay, then."

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.400
"Okay, then."

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.600
"Okay, then."

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.980
By telling a story about people who lash&nbsp;
out and hurt one another for no reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:28.679
"Fargo" Season 2 explores the limits&nbsp;
of what we can know about human nature.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.340
While both a serious drama and a compelling&nbsp;
comedy, "Fleabag" Season 2 is also a love story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:40.800
The 1st season followed Phoebe Waller-Bridge's&nbsp;
titular character as she excavated the traumas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.540
of her past and her own role in creating them.&nbsp;
In Season 2, Fleabag attempts to move forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:51.180
and ultimately finds herself attracted to&nbsp;
a Catholic priest. The two consummate their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.400
relationship before the priest decides that&nbsp;
his dedication must remain with his faith.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.360
"Fleabag" is funnier and&nbsp;
sharper in its 2nd season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:06.720
but it's every bit as devastating as it was in&nbsp;
its initial run. As these episodes make clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.340
the fourth-wall-breaking that has been&nbsp;
a core part of the show's style is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.060
a coping mechanism for the show's main&nbsp;
character. Fleabag talks to the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.080
and the audience to distance herself from&nbsp;
the world immediately in front of her.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.240
"Cheers, may these be the worst of our days!"

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.060
"Don't know who this guy is."

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:30.300
In the season's final moments, though, she leaves&nbsp;
the camera behind, ready to lead a life in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.940
she's fully engaged with the people around her.&nbsp;
She may have lost out on the handsome priest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.326
but her ill-starred love for him&nbsp;
gave her maturity and humility.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:46.200
Laura Palmer is dead. That's the simple&nbsp;
premise of "Twin Peaks," one that horrifies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.940
creators David Lynch and Mark Frost as much&nbsp;
as it enthralls them. Across its 1st season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.800
"Twin Peaks" aired 8 nearly perfect&nbsp;
episodes that were focused on finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.000
Laura's killer and discovering the&nbsp;
town of Twin Peaks' seedy underbelly.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.520
Across those eight hours, "Twin Peaks" combined&nbsp;
elements of a murder investigation with small-town&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:11.460
drama, science fiction, and even a few helpful&nbsp;
handfuls of soap opera storytelling. As with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:18.000
much of Lynch's work, the tone is nightmarishly&nbsp;
surreal and incredibly stylish, complicating what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.400
at first glance might seem like a standard police&nbsp;
procedural. Twin Peaks feels like a normal town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.260
but it's hiding a darkness as deep&nbsp;
and terrifying as the pits of hell.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:34.860
Although the show stumbled in its later seasons,&nbsp;
the 1st season alone made "Twin Peaks" one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:40.920
most important and impactful TV series of&nbsp;
all time. Decades after its 1990 air date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.060
it still feels like audiences aren't fully&nbsp;
ready for what the show has to offer.

00:06:46.397 --> 00:06:51.780
"Homeland" rode the wave of rave reviews&nbsp;
for its 1st season into its 2nd. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:55.140
the critical reception to the show&nbsp;
eventually became less rapturous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:01.620
Season 2 proved to be a high watermark. The&nbsp;
show follows Carrie, a CIA agent with bipolar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.060
disorder who becomes convinced that a&nbsp;
returning war veteran named Brody has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:10.380
been turned by al-Qaeda. After Brody fails&nbsp;
to detonate a bomb meant to assasinate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:15.180
vice president at the end of Season 1, Season&nbsp;
two deals with the fallout from his inaction.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:19.680
Carrie and Brody eventually strike up&nbsp;
a relationship, which only complicates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:25.800
Carrie's place inside the CIA. The espionage on&nbsp;
"Homeland" is a huge part of the show's appeal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:29.460
but the relationships between its&nbsp;
characters are what make the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.500
feel truly lived-in. "Homeland" was a&nbsp;
phenomenon upon its initial release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:40.646
and the reviews for Season 2 proved that&nbsp;
it was a series with some staying power.

00:07:40.646 --> 00:07:43.200
Season 2 of "The Sopranos" was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.940
build off of the excitement that many critics had&nbsp;
after the show's incredible first season. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:53.580
some have come to see the 2nd season as a bit of&nbsp;
a letdown, it's also received plenty of praise.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:58.500
In the 2nd season, Tony discovers that one&nbsp;
of his closest friends is an FBI informant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:03.900
and he continues to see Dr. Melfi in spite&nbsp;
of her increasing disgust over what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.580
does for a living. Meanwhile, Tony's sister&nbsp;
returns to his life, and she and Tony must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.880
confront their complicated relationship&nbsp;
with each other and with their mother.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:16.860
The 2nd season of "The Sopranos" is every bit as&nbsp;
strong as the 1st, even if it lacks some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:21.600
shock factor that made those initial episodes&nbsp;
so celebrated. With its second installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:28.239
"The Sopranos" solidified its status as superb&nbsp;
television and proved it was in for the long haul.

00:08:28.239 --> 00:08:32.760
By the time "The Sopranos" was in its&nbsp;
3rd season, it had cemented itself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.620
one of the best TV shows on air, and&nbsp;
possibly of all time. In Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:43.200
the show dives even deeper into Tony's psyche&nbsp;
and the reason for his continued panic attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.580
and we get deeper insights into Tony's&nbsp;
relationships with his children. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:53.700
season also gave fans one of the best—and&nbsp;
hardest to watch—episodes of the entire series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:59.280
in which Dr. Melfi is understandably tempted to&nbsp;
make use of her client's extrajudicial power.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:05.220
The genius of "The Sopranos" was the way the show&nbsp;
managed to put equal emphasis on every aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.960
its character's lives. The mob storylines&nbsp;
were as compelling as ever, and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:15.060
only made more interesting by the insight the&nbsp;
show provided into Tony's life outside of work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:20.760
Throughout the show, there's little separation&nbsp;
between Tony's family and his criminal activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:26.220
and as his children grow up and attempt to chart&nbsp;
their own courses, that only becomes more evident.

00:09:26.534 --> 00:09:28.274
"Is mom there?"

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.000
"Yeah, she's here. How's school?"

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.459
"Fine, is mom there?"

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.500
Given the fact that it's widely regarded&nbsp;
as one of the great TV dramas of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.880
it should come as little surprise that&nbsp;
several seasons of "The Sopranos" made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.840
it onto this list. While it's common for&nbsp;
popular shows to taper out near the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.380
"The Sopranos" stayed strong&nbsp;
right up to its finale.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:56.280
The controversy over the finale&nbsp;
tends to overshadow the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:01.440
Season 6 was consistently great long&nbsp;
before that abrupt cut to black. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.400
6th season was split into two parts,&nbsp;
with 12 episodes airing in one block,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:10.080
followed by an additional 9-episode&nbsp;
run to conclude the entire series.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:15.480
Many of the members of Tony's criminal&nbsp;
organization seem to be on a path towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.860
redemption, but they're all ultimately&nbsp;
pulled back into the violent life of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:24.300
crime they've been leading all along. They&nbsp;
may believe that they're capable of change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:29.760
but as the final season shows, becoming a&nbsp;
new person is much harder than it seems.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.360
"We're soldiers, ya know?.&nbsp;
Soldiers don't go to hell."

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:35.940
By the time Season 3 rolled around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:42.180
"The Leftovers" was the definition of a critical&nbsp;
darling. After a divisive first season and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:47.280
critically adored second, expectations were high&nbsp;
for the third installment. Luckily for showrunners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:52.740
and fans alike, the series' third season&nbsp;
delivered. Set seven years after the departure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:58.260
that launched the series, Season 3 focuses on the&nbsp;
show's core characters as they reckon with whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:03.240
or not they've actually evolved in the years since&nbsp;
two percent of the world's population vanished.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.940
The show is just as strange as ever in Season&nbsp;
3, which shifts much of the action to Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:13.380
as some characters begin to suspect that&nbsp;
the end of the world is imminent. In just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:17.940
eight short episodes, the show manages to&nbsp;
tie off many of its central relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:23.160
and also provides glimmers of hope for characters&nbsp;
who have spent much of the series totally lost.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.846
"The world is ending."

00:11:24.846 --> 00:11:30.960
In a brutal, perfect finale, we see the character&nbsp;
of Nora living alone in the distant future after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.760
attempting to reunite with the family she lost&nbsp;
on Departure Day. The episode is beautiful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:42.660
haunting, and lovely, and it's a great showcase&nbsp;
for leading actors Justin Theroux and Carrie Coon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:47.801
who give two exceptional performances&nbsp;
as the show's broken, desperate leads.

00:11:47.801 --> 00:11:54.060
After an incredible first season, "The Wire"&nbsp;
returned for a second season that dazzled critics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:58.440
With a focus on the effects broken and corrupt&nbsp;
systems have on the lives of its characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:02.460
Season 2 depicted the ways in which the&nbsp;
dock workers were ultimately connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:07.380
to the drug trade and to an international crime&nbsp;
syndicate that flourished as a result. It also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:11.820
achieved the rare distinction of making a&nbsp;
compelling drama out of the seemingly dry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:15.840
subject matter of union politics like&nbsp;
seniority rights and decertification.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:20.400
The 2nd season of "The Wire" also takes&nbsp;
a hard look at the decline of the working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:25.860
class, showing how the changing world has&nbsp;
destabilized labor. As the world moves forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:30.900
many essential workers get left behind.&nbsp;
"The Wire" was about one city's problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:35.460
but they often felt so universal that&nbsp;
anyone could empathize with them.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:40.800
Considered by critics to be the pinnacle of the&nbsp;
show's quality, Season three of "The Wire" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:45.300
a lot to offer. The season's central premise is&nbsp;
the creation of safe zones throughout the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:50.460
of Baltimore where drug activity is legal,&nbsp;
a situation hilariously dubbed "Hamsterdam."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:55.320
It's a dangerous, high-stakes approach to&nbsp;
crime management that seems irresponsible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.320 --> 00:13:00.300
but ultimately leads to lower levels of violence&nbsp;
in the district where the rules are put in place.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:06.180
Ultimately, though, those safe zones are exposed,&nbsp;
and politicians use their existence to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:11.220
grandstanding speeches. Meanwhile, the drug war&nbsp;
continues unabated across the rest of the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:16.620
and violence escalates to the point where Stringer&nbsp;
Bell is killed by Omar Little and Brother Mouzone.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:22.140
The season is a masterpiece of carefully plotted&nbsp;
story, one that imagines a brighter future for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:26.700
Baltimore that always seems to be just out&nbsp;
of reach. While "The Wire" never shied away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:31.740
from the systemic nature of the problems it&nbsp;
depicted, Season 3 also showed how decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:37.080
made by the people in those systems can&nbsp;
both reinforce and untangle those problems.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:42.620
"You going back to your homes tonight,&nbsp;
we gonna be in our patrol cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:45.540
and them boys still gonna be&nbsp;
out there on them corners."

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.980
The fourth season of "The Wire"&nbsp;
may be the most heartbreaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:54.420
of all. Each season of the show focused&nbsp;
on a different institution within urban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:58.680
Baltimore. Season 4 turned the camera&nbsp;
on Baltimore's youth and the underfunded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.980
school system that tries desperately&nbsp;
to keep them from turning to crime.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:07.260
It's tough to watch how the system slowly corrupts&nbsp;
innocent children, but it gives audiences a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:11.700
picture of exactly how broken the world of "The&nbsp;
Wire" is while shedding light on how closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:16.080
the show mirrors reality. The seasons' other&nbsp;
major storyline, which involves the primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:20.940
race for Baltimore mayor, establishes how the&nbsp;
political machine stifles any attempt at reform.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:25.260
Thomas Carcetti ascends to the office of&nbsp;
mayor intending to change the systems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:30.300
have trapped so many people in difficult&nbsp;
circumstances, but finds himself unable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:36.434
deliver. The frank look at municipal dysfunction&nbsp;
gets leavened by moments of gallows humor.

00:14:36.434 --> 00:14:38.400
When "Breaking Bad" starts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:43.980
Walter White is an average guy. Maybe he's got&nbsp;
some anger issues, and maybe he feels stifled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:48.840
by his job as a high school chemistry teacher,&nbsp;
but he's a dedicated family man. That changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:53.820
when he gets a cancer diagnosis, setting off a&nbsp;
chain of events that leads him to start making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.820 --> 00:15:00.120
and selling meth. By Season 4, Walt is still&nbsp;
alive, but he's become an irredeemable monster.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:04.500
Season 4 is so well-regarded in part&nbsp;
because it's the season where Walt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:09.240
fully embraces this transformation as&nbsp;
he attempts to take down Gus Fring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:14.820
the show's main villain. Walt wins his battle&nbsp;
with Gus, but it comes at the soul-corrupting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:20.880
cost of poisoning a child and planting bombs&nbsp;
on an old man's wheelchair. These measures make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:25.680
it clear that, by the end of Season 4, Walt&nbsp;
is willing to do anything to achieve power.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.200
Aside from featuring a myriad of iconic&nbsp;
scenes, Season 4 plumbed the depths of each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:37.299
of its characters, producing both tense drama&nbsp;
and a particularly dark shade of black comedy.

00:15:37.299 --> 00:15:40.615
*Walt laughing*

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:47.400
"Breaking Bad" went out on top. In its 5th and&nbsp;
final season, Walt is finally making all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:52.680
calls. He's the head of his own meth empire,&nbsp;
and he's working with Mike and Jesse to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:57.600
one of the major forces in the southwest drug&nbsp;
trade. Much like "The Sopranos," the first half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:03.120
of this split season depicts a near-meteoric rise&nbsp;
for Walt in the aftermath of Gus Fring's death.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:08.460
Ultimately, though, that rise can't last. Walt's&nbsp;
brother-in-law, Hank, discovers that Walt is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:13.200
elusive meth dealer he's been tracking for&nbsp;
years. Hank's unraveling of Walt's secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:18.420
ultimately leads to Walt's downfall, as his&nbsp;
family loses everything and he's forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:23.820
go on the run. Jesse winds up imprisoned by a&nbsp;
group of neo-Nazis, where he's forced to cook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:29.340
meth or be cruelly tortured. In the end, Walt&nbsp;
finds some measure of redemption and humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:34.860
by sacrificing himself to save Jesse. He also&nbsp;
finally admits to his wife, Skyler, that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:40.860
the horrible things he's done was for himself and&nbsp;
not a selfless mission to provide for his family.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:45.120
Season 5 is a bleak masterpiece,&nbsp;
serving as a reminder that all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:47.820
terrible things audiences have&nbsp;
watched Walt do over the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:51.480
of the show will eventually come back&nbsp;
to haunt him and those he cares about.

00:16:52.572 --> 00:16:53.640
"Rectify" is a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:58.260
about broken people working to make themselves&nbsp;
whole. The show follows a man who spent nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:03.720
20 years on death row for a crime he didn’t&nbsp;
commit. DNA evidence ultimately exonerates him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:06.840
and he's released from prison.&nbsp;
When he returns to his hometown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:11.520
he finds it a strange place totally&nbsp;
unlike the one he left, and he's forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:15.180
to reckon with all of the relationships&nbsp;
he left behind when he was incarcerated.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:21.120
Through every season, and especially in its&nbsp;
4th and final outing, "Rectify" was a quiet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:24.480
deeply moving character drama.&nbsp;
It never overplayed its hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:29.580
and it allowed its characters to grow and change&nbsp;
as the episodes progressed. "Rectify" may not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:34.080
remembered as one of the best TV shows of&nbsp;
all time, but that's because it has none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:39.360
of the flair or excitement that defined many&nbsp;
of the series on this list. Instead, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:45.300
happy to be subtly contemplative and beautiful&nbsp;
in ways that most TV shows rarely try to be.

00:17:46.106 --> 00:17:49.800
"Genndy Tartakovsky's Primal"&nbsp;
is clearly a long-simmering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:55.020
delicately-executed passion project for&nbsp;
its creator, an animation titan who looms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.020 --> 00:18:00.600
so large over his world that his name is in the&nbsp;
title. The series is one of the most ambitious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:06.600
visually deep, and gorgeous TV cartoons&nbsp;
in decades. An epic fantasy quest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:11.220
"Primal" feels more reminiscent of a&nbsp;
mystical saga from a novel, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:16.080
an Adult Swim show from the person who conceived&nbsp;
"Dexter's Laboratory" and "Hotel Transylvania."

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:20.880
"So what do you want a cookie? You&nbsp;
did alright, man. Move on already."

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:27.420
Deliberately historically inaccurate to maximize&nbsp;
drama and possibility, "Primal" takes place in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:33.300
distant prehistoric time period. Here, humans,&nbsp;
earlier evolutions of humankind, and dinosaurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:38.340
all live among each other fighting to survive&nbsp;
and thrive. The action centers on Spear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:43.860
a Neanderthal and caveman left alone in a brutal&nbsp;
world when a pack of dinosaurs kills his family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:48.780
He befriends a small dino named Fang whose&nbsp;
family was killed by the same vicious clan.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:54.420
They form a de facto unit and a strong bond, which&nbsp;
fuels the action in the almost critically-perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.420 --> 00:19:00.060
Season 2 of "Primal." Spear and Fang face&nbsp;
all sorts of internal and external conflicts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:04.560
from a giant shark to more advanced&nbsp;
groups of humans — and the animation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:08.520
team behind it all was up to the&nbsp;
task. Tartakovsky told Indiewire,

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:13.200
"There are a number of episodes where there&nbsp;
are eight to 10-minute fight sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:16.926
and each one of those almost broke the studio."

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:23.940
"Atlanta" is a thoughtful, meditative, surreal&nbsp;
look at modern life, particularly for young Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:28.440
men balancing artistic pursuits with the need&nbsp;
to make enough money to pull themselves out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:34.680
poverty in a hostile world. It's a dazzling series&nbsp;
that changes its tone as the story requires, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:41.280
each season and episode wildly different from the&nbsp;
last. Created, chiefly written by, and starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:46.560
comedian-actor-musician Donald Glover, "Atlanta"&nbsp;
routinely churns out 30-minute masterpieces.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:51.240
According to "Metacritic", the best of these&nbsp;
fall within Season 2 of the show. This season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:55.860
is subtitled "Robbin' Season" to reflect&nbsp;
a certain desperation felt by the extended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.860 --> 00:20:00.780
universe of characters in this barely fictional&nbsp;
representation of the titular Southern metropolis.

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:07.320
The final part of the final season of the final&nbsp;
chapter of the "Breaking Bad" saga certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:11.640
stuck the landing. Bob Odenkirk joined the&nbsp;
cast of "Breaking Bad" midway through its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:16.200
run as Saul Goodman, a sleazy bus-bench&nbsp;
lawyer who helps with the legal issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:21.000
of the show's various drug manufacturing and&nbsp;
distributing characters. He was so compelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:25.320
AMC commissioned the spinoff "Better Call&nbsp;
Saul," The "Breaking Bad" prequel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:28.200
showed how small-time hustler&nbsp;
and part-time public defender&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:33.540
Jimmy McGill evolved into a swaggering&nbsp;
wheeler-dealer of questionable morality.

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:39.660
By Season 6.5 of "Better Call Saul," the&nbsp;
transformation from Jimmy to Saul nears its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:44.820
completion, while the rest of the "Breaking Bad"&nbsp;
backstory also gets filled in. Character-driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:49.980
and empathetic, the final slew of "Better&nbsp;
Call Saul" episodes also give quite a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:56.040
glimpses into what became of Jimmy after he had&nbsp;
to abandon his life and identity as Saul Goodman.

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:57.886
"Look at that."

00:20:57.886 --> 00:21:03.900
It seems Saul mostly lives his life alone,&nbsp;
depressed, and working at a Cinnabon. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:08.580
the last few episodes show us it doesn't&nbsp;
stay that way forever. Saul starts up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:13.646
his old scams again, and all of a sudden,&nbsp;
his old life begins to catch up with him.

00:21:13.646 --> 00:21:17.400
Based on acclaimed Italian novelist&nbsp;
Elena Ferrante's books of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:21.840
"My Brilliant Friend" was produced for&nbsp;
Italian television but airs in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:26.340
on HBO. Ferrante has co-written every&nbsp;
episode of the critically lauded series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:30.780
which tells a story of a triumph-and-tragedy&nbsp;
filled friendship between two girls from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.900
a poor neighborhood near Naples&nbsp;
who met as children in the 1950s.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:37.020
At the outset of Season 3,&nbsp;
it's the dawn of the 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:40.440
and Lila and Elena have taken&nbsp;
very different paths into their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:45.480
young adulthoods. Lila is struggling,&nbsp;
having married at 17, birthed a child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:50.400
and left her husband. She finds herself working&nbsp;
in a meat processing factory to support herself.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:56.220
Meanwhile, Elena has left Naples, become a famous&nbsp;
author, and lives comfortably among the wealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.220 --> 00:22:01.860
literary elite. As each season tells a different&nbsp;
part of the long saga, and is set in a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:07.260
era, the filmic nature of "My Brilliant Friend"&nbsp;
changes. Season 3 embraces the aesthetic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:12.300
its 1970s settings, emulating the look and&nbsp;
feels of American indie movies of the era.

00:22:12.981 --> 00:22:16.620
"Murder One" is not as well known&nbsp;
as many of the series on this list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:21.420
but that doesn't mean the show wasn't&nbsp;
influential. Lasting for only two seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:26.940
"Murder One" was created by Steven Bochco&nbsp;
and broke the mold when it debuted in 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:32.520
focusing the entirety of its 1st season on a&nbsp;
single criminal case. In heavily serializing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:36.480
its story, "Murder One" helped to more&nbsp;
firmly establish the idea that viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:41.040
would be interested in the minutiae of a&nbsp;
case as it winds its way towards a trial.

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:43.440
Paired with award-winning cinematography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:47.820
by filmmake Aaron Schneider, "Murder&nbsp;
One"'s formula-breaking plot managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.960
to make every detail of the criminal&nbsp;
case more fascinating than the last.

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:58.380
As a documentary, "Planet Earth II" might not&nbsp;
seem like it belongs on this list of dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:03.060
but let's trust Metacritic on this one.&nbsp;
There's only one season of "Planet Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:07.320
II," but this sequel to the original&nbsp;
"Planet Earth" proves that nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:11.700
documentaries don't have to be dry museum&nbsp;
material. Narrated by David Attenborough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:15.900
"Planet Earth II" is an ode to the&nbsp;
wildlife that exists all over the planet.

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:21.900
The footage is breathtaking and remarkably&nbsp;
vivid, and it serves as a reminder that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:27.180
world contains beauty that doesn't require any&nbsp;
sort of computer-generated enhancement. The 2016&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:33.300
miniseries, which was released 10 years after its&nbsp;
predecessor, also serves as an urgent reminder of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:38.760
all that the world stands to lose if nothing is&nbsp;
done to combat the threat of industrialization and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:44.580
climate change. The documentary isn't explicitly&nbsp;
political, but merely by documenting the natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:50.100
beauty of the ecosystems and wildlife around&nbsp;
us, the film highlights their imperiled state.

